oblem); (iii) if the equation cannot be verified offline, the BAdam arm is **BLOCKED** \u2014 do not guess.\n\n## 4. Setup\n- **Model:** GPT-2 small (124M; 12 layers; GELU MLP), pretrained checkpoint, full-parameter finetuning.\n- **Modules for SVD analysis** (per layer \u00d712, grouped by type): attention `c_attn` (768\u00d73072), output projection `c_proj` (768\u00d7768), MLP `c_fc` (768\u00d73072); plus embedding `wte` (50257\u00d7768; LM head is weight-tied to it in GPT-2, so it is the same matrix). LayerNorms (elementwise) excluded from SVD, reported descriptively. This covers 4 module types \u2014 the \"various transformer modules\" axis.\n- **Data:** a fixed plain-text corpus subset (e.g., ~2M tokens from WikiText-2/BookCorpus) **pre-provisioned** (network disabled at runtime). Fallback if no corpus is provisioned: a deterministic synthetic token corpus with fixed seed, flagged as reducing external validity.\n- **Schedule:** batch 8, seq 128, 1000 steps, 100-step linear warmup, cosine decay, \u03b7 = 5e-5, \u03b21 = 0.9, \u03b22 = 0.999, \u03b5 = 1e-8 \u2014 identical for both optimizers.\n- **Seeds:** 3 (minimum; 5 preferred). Within each seed, both optimizers use the **same checkpoint and same data order** (shared init/seed control).\n\n## 5. Operational definitions and rationale (each tied to the question)\n| Measurement | Definition | Why this choice answers the question |\n|---|---|---|\n| Learning perturbation (primary) | \u0394W = W(t=T) \u2212 W(t=0) per module | The question asks about the *learning perturbation* \u2014 the net parameter change \u2014 of finetuning; the accumulated window is the canonical object whose spectrum the BAdam literature characterizes |\n| Learning perturbation (control) | Single-step \u0394W_t = W(t)\u2212W(t\u22121), singular values computed online at t = 50, 100, \u2026, 1000 (20 samples) | Tests that the heavy-tail signature is not an artifact of accumulation; cheap (store singular values only, not matrices) |\n| Singular value distribution | Full spectrum \u03c3\u2081\u2265\u2026\u2265\u03c3\u1d63 of each \u0394W via SVD | The question is about the *distribution* of singular values, so the full spectrum is analyzed, not just top-k |\n| Heavy-tail metric | Power-law fit \u03c3_i \u221d i^{\u2212\u03b1} on the rank-ordered spectrum; fit range set by the MLE procedure (A5); report \u03b1, R\u00b2, KS goodness-of-fit p-value, and log-likelihood ratio vs. an exponential null | A heavy tail is operationally \"significantly slower decay than exponential\"; the MLE range rule (A5) prevents range-cherry-picking, and the exponential null is the natural non-heavy-tailed alternative |\n| Heavy-tail null control | Gaussian random matrix with matched Frobenius norm (same shape), spectrum of its \u0394W-analogue | Calibrates \"heavy-tailed\" against a baseline spectrum that is *not* heavy-tailed; heavy tail = BAdam spectrum decays significantly slower than this null |\n| Effective rank (primary) | effrank = exp(\u2212\u03a3 p\u1d62 ln p\u1d62), p\u1d62 = \u03c3\u1d62\u00b2/\u03a3\u03c3\u2c7c\u00b2 (A4) | Standard scale-invariant effective rank; directly comparable across module shapes and optimizers |\n| Effective rank (cross-checks) | \u03b5-rank: count of \u03c3\u1d62 \u2265 1e-3\u00b7\u03c3\u2081; and \u03c3-based entropy rank (q\u1d62 = \u03c3\u1d62/\u03a3\u03c3\u2c7c). Also report effrank/r (r = min(dims)) | Guards against the conclusion being an artifact of one rank definition (control C4) |\n\n## 6. Conditions and controls\n- **C1 \u2014 Adam baseline, matched hyperparameters:** identical \u03b7, \u03b21, \u03b22, \u03b5, schedule, data, batch, seq length; only the optimizer update rule differs.\n- **C2 \u2014 Shared init/seed:** same pretrained checkpoint and same data shuffle order per seed for both optimizers.\n- **C3 \u2014 Single-step vs accumulated \u0394W:** per \u00a75; heavy tail must be consistent across both (accumulated primary; single-step must not contradict).\n- **C4 \u2014 \u03b5-rank cross-check:** verdict on H2 requires primary and cross-check ranks to agree in direction.\n- **C5 \u2014 Gaussian null spectrum:** per \u00a75, per module shape.\n- **C6 \u2014 Performance sanity check:** both optimizers must reach comparable validation loss (within a small margin) over the window; otherwise the \u0394W comparison is confounded by one optimizer simply not learning, and this is reported as a confound.\n\n## 7. Analysis plan and decision criteria\n1. **Per-module spectra:** log-log plot of \u03c3\u1d62 vs rank for each module type (attn / out-proj / MLP / embedding), both optimizers, all seeds; overlay the Gaussian null.\n2. **Tail index \u03b1 with CIs:** MLE fit per matrix; CI via parametric bootstrap of the power-law model (e.g., 200 resamples) plus across-seed dispersion; report \u03b1 \u00b1 CI per module type.\n3. **Effective rank:** per module, per seed, per optimizer; paired log-ratios across modules\u00d7seeds.\n4. **Paired statistics:** paired Wilcoxon signed-rank (primary, robust) and paired t-test on log(effrank) across the set of module-matrices within each seed, aggregated over seeds; report mean ratio, median ratio, 95% CI.\n5. **Decision criteria:**\n   - **H1 \"heavy-tailed\" (BAdam, per module type):** all of (a) power-law fit R\u00b2 \u2265 0.9 within the MLE fit range; (b) KS goodness-of-fit p \u2265 0.05 (power law not rejected); (c) LRT vs exponential null significant (p < 0.05); (d) \u03b1 in the heavy-tail-plausible range (0 < \u03b1 < 2); (e) spectrum decays significantly slower than the Gaussian null control. Verdict: YES/NO per module type.\n   - **H2 \"comparable effective rank\":** per-module ratio R = effrank_BAdam/effrank_Adam. *Comparable* iff the median R across module-matrices and seeds lies in **[0.5, 2.0]** (a factor-of-2 band) **and** the 95% CI of the mean log-ratio excludes \u00b1ln 2. Cross-check (C4) must agree in direction. Verdict: COMPARABLE / HIGHER / LOWER.\n   - **Overall answer:** conjunction table (module type \u00d7 {heavy-tailed?, rank verdict}), plus the C6 confound check.\n\n## 8. Feasibility (CPU-only)\n- **Throughput gate:** before main runs, do a 50-step pilot to measure GFLOP/s per run; expected ~0.76 TFLOP per step (6\u00b7N\u00b7tokens, N=124M, 1024 tokens/step) \u2192 ~7.6e14 FLOPs per 1000-step run. At a conservative 300 GFLOP/s multi-core sustained \u2192 ~7 h/run; at 1 TFLOP/s \u2192 ~25 min/run. Budget: **\u2264 48 h wall-clock total** for 6\u201310 runs (3\u20135 seeds \u00d7 2 optimizers) on 224 cores; if the pilot is slower, scale down to a ~60M model or 500 steps (heavy-tail signatures are expected to be stable to window length, to be checked via C3).\n- **Memory:** 124M params \u00d7 (weights + 2 Adam states + grads) \u2248 2\u20134 GB \u2014 trivial vs ~2 TB available.\n- **Storage:** store singular values only (36 matrices \u00d7 1000 steps \u00d7 \u2264768 floats \u2248 <0.3 GB per run) \u2014 trivial.\n- **SVD cost:** largest matrix 768\u00d73072 \u2014 seconds each, negligible.\n- **Install assumptions (A6):** torch-CPU and numpy wheels must be pre-provisioned because runtime network is disabled. **Risk:** if no wheels are available, a pure-Python fallback (hand-rolled transformer + SVD) is only feasible at very small scale and is a last resort; the design's dependency list must be checked at execution time and the run BLOCKED (not silently downgraded) if SVD-grade numerics are unavailable.\n\n## 9. Procedure (protocol)\n1. Provision wheels; verify BAdam equation (c_t) against source; pass implementation gate (\u00a73, blocking).\n2. Provision corpus (or synthetic fallback, flagged); freeze checkpoint, data order, hyperparameters.\n3. Run 50-step pilot; confirm throughput within \u00a78 budget.\n4. Main runs: seeds \u00d7 {Adam, BAdam}, 1000 steps each; log per-module \u0394W singular values (final + sampled single-step), validation loss (C6).\n5. Analysis pipeline per \u00a77; produce verdict table + spectrum plots + confidence-interval tables.\n6. Report: verdicts per module type, confound status (C6), and scope caveats (A2, A1).\n\n## 10. Limitations (to state in any report)\nSmall-model scale (A2); single corpus/domain; 3\u20135 seeds (power for tail-index differences is unproven at this n); full finetuning only; BAdam equation pending source verification (A1); \"comparable\" band [0.5, 2.0] is a proposed convention, not a literature standard.", "summary": "Deliver full final experiment design as result", "evidence": "Repository inspection confirmed /workspace holds only task.json (question + context \"Do not claim that an experiment was run\") and track.md (\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\") \u2014 no code, configs, data, or reference material, so the design specifies all implementation details itself. Environment probe: Python 3.14.4, torch/numpy absent, network disabled, GPU disabled, 224 cores, ~2 TB RAM \u2014 which fixed the CPU-only feasibility budget, the pre-provisioned-wheels assumption (A6), and the \u2264124M model scope (A2). The approved plan (previous turn) fixed the measurement choices: accumulated \u0394W = W_final \u2212 W_init as primary perturbation with single-step \u0394W_t as control, full-spectrum SVD with MLE-range power-law fit + exponential null for heavy tail, entropy-of-normalized-\u03c3\u00b2 effective rank with \u03b5-rank cross-check, paired same-init/same-seed Adam-vs-BAdam runs, and GPT-2-style module grouping. This deliverable extends that with the BAdam update-rule transcription (flagged for source verification with a blocking implementation gate), explicit decision thresholds (R\u00b2\u22650.9, KS p\u22650.05, LRT p<0.05, \u03b1\u2208(0,2), rank-ratio band [0.5,2.0] with CI excluding \u00b1ln 2), the Gaussian-null spectrum control, the performance-sanity control, and the throughput-gated 48-hour CPU budget.", "assumptions": ["A1: BAdam = bias-reduced Adam (Wang et al. 2024); the correction factor c_t is transcribed structurally and MUST be verified verbatim against the source at execution time (blocking implementation gate)", "A2: small-model (\u2264124M) scope is an accepted methodological choice for the 'various transformer modules' claim", "A3: full-parameter finetuning from a pretrained checkpoint, no adapters", "A4: effective rank = exp(entropy of normalized squared singular values), with \u03b5-rank (1e-3) and \u03c3-based entropy rank as cross-checks", "A5: power-law fit range chosen by the MLE (Clauset\u2013Shalizi\u2013Newman style) procedure", "A6: torch-CPU and numpy wheels are pre-provisioned at execution time (runtime network disabled); runs are blocked, not silently downgraded, if unavailable", "3\u20135 seeds; 1000 steps, batch 8, seq 128, \u03b7=5e-5, \u03b2=(0.9,0.999), \u03b5=1e-8 \u2014 proposed values, adjustable via the throughput gate", "Comparable-rank band [0.5, 2.0] and all H1 thresholds are proposed conventions, not literature standards"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-5ade8081daa04447a372195a3741b818", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Deliver full final experiment design as result", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
